    "q-80643": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80643",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2, \\\\ {f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(a{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+6xy+2x) \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =2a{x}^{2}y+6x \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(b{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x+0 \\\\  & =3b{x}^{2}y+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a{x}^{2}y+6x=3b{x}^{2}y+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of ${x}^{2}y$, we see that $a=\\frac{3b}{2}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{y}(-1,1)=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,1) & =3 \\\\ b(-1)^{3}(1)+3(-1)^{2}+2 & =3 \\\\ -b+3+2 & =3 \\\\ b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na=\\frac{3b}{2}=3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
