    "q-133295": {
      "dom_id": "step-q133295",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-y}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-y})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
