    "q-80831": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80831",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${f}_{xyy}(2,2,2)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{yx}(x,y,z)=2y\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{3}}{x}}+3{z}^{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyy}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{yxy}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{yx}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{3}}{x}}+3{z}^{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{3}}{x}}+3{z}^{3}\\sqrt{x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{{z}^{3}}{x}}-\\frac{3}{2}{z}^{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{{y}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyy}(2,2,2) & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{(2)^{3}}{(2)}}-\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{(2)}{(2)^{3}}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
