    "q-80840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80840",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zx}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,{f}_{yz}(x,y,z)=\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,2,1):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z)+{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{yzy}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{yz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{2xz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxx}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zx}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\frac{2yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(x,y,z)+{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)=-\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}+\\frac{4xyz}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{f}_{zyy}(1,2,1)+{f}_{xxz}(1,2,1)=0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
