    "q-65133": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65133",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yx}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1?$",
      "worked_solution": "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)={f}_{xxy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xxy}(1,0) & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+{f}_{xyx}(1,0)-{f}_{xyx}(1,0) \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0)+\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)}-\\cancel{{f}_{xxy}(1,0)} \\\\  & ={f}_{yx}(1,0) \\\\  & =2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0)-(1)^{2}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot 0)+1 \\\\  & =2-0+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
